# 💾 System Images for Bumblebee

This folder contains Raspberry Pi system images for the Bumblebee drone.

Flash the image to an SD card (16 GB or larger) and insert it into the drone's Raspberry Pi 5 — no additional setup required. The image ships Ubuntu 24.04 with ROS 2 Jazzy and the full pre-built Bumblebee stack (login `lb` / `lb`). Current version: **1.0.1** (`bumblebee-1.0.1-rpi5.img.xz`, ~2.0 GB, 8.4 GB unpacked), one image for every drone.

Available images and download links: see [download.md](download.md).

For full flashing instructions and first-boot steps:
👉 **[SD Card Image — Bumblebee Students Wiki](https://github.com/futureLabKezad/bumblebee-students/wiki/SD‐Card‐Image)**

## Flashing the Image

Use **[Raspberry Pi Imager](https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/)**: *Device* → Raspberry Pi 5, *Operating System* → **Use custom** → pick `bumblebee-1.0.1-rpi5.img.xz` (no need to unpack), *Storage* → your SD card. When asked about OS customisation, answer **No** — the image sets itself up.

Or from the command line:

```bash
# Flash straight from the .img.xz (replace /dev/sdX with your SD card)
xz -dc bumblebee-1.0.1-rpi5.img.xz | sudo dd of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress conv=fsync
```

## First boot

- The root partition grows to fill the card, and the drone generates its own SSH host keys and machine-id.
- The drone names itself after its Wi-Fi MAC: `Bumblebee-XXXX` (hostname `bumblebee-xxxx`, reachable as `bumblebee-xxxx.local`).
- With no known Wi-Fi in range it raises its hotspot `Bumblebee-XXXX` (password `12345678`, drone at `10.42.0.1`). In the ground station app press **Add drone** to send it your Wi-Fi.

Before 1.0.1 the image was `V1.0.0-beta` (`.img.gz`): every drone flashed from it had the same name (`Bumblebee-4`), SSH keys and machine-id. It stays available for rollback (see [download.md](download.md)).

The release image is built reproducibly from the V1.0.0-beta base by `drone/image/make_release_image.sh` and checked by `drone/image/verify_release_image.sh` (82 checks).
